speaker 1: HeatWave MySQL sounds different from a plain managed MySQL service, just from the name.
speaker 2: Right, its biggest feature is a built-in in-memory query acceleration engine called HeatWave, letting you run analytical queries directly within the same MySQL instance.
speaker 1: What problem does this actually solve?
speaker 2: In a traditional architecture, if you want to do transactions and analytics simultaneously, you'd usually run transactions on Aurora MySQL and sync data to Redshift for analysis via ETL - which adds latency, extra cost, and more architectural complexity.
speaker 1: How does HeatWave MySQL simplify that flow?
speaker 2: It keeps both transaction processing and analytical query capability inside the same instance - no separate ETL pipeline needed. As soon as data is written, it's directly queryable by the analytics engine.
speaker 1: Does this mean I could drop the Redshift layer entirely?
speaker 2: In many scenarios, yes - especially for teams whose analytics needs aren't especially heavy and who want to keep the architecture simple, this combination becomes appealing.
speaker 1: If my analytics needs are especially complex and data volume is especially large, would I still need a dedicated data warehouse?
speaker 2: Right, in sufficiently complex scenarios a dedicated data warehouse product might still be more suitable. HeatWave MySQL's advantage mainly shows up when simplifying medium-scale real-time analytics needs.
speaker 1: If my current architecture is Aurora MySQL plus Redshift, would it be worth migrating?
speaker 2: Whether it's worth it depends on your ETL pipeline complexity and maintenance cost. If you're already struggling with sync latency and ETL maintenance overhead, this service is worth evaluating.
